# Who may sign tags in this repository, and with which key.
#
# Point git at this file and `git verify-tag` and `git log --show-signature`
# start working against it:
#
#     git config gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile .github/allowed_signers
#     git verify-tag v0.12.0
#
# GitHub verifies signatures on its own and shows a Verified badge either way.
# This file is what lets someone who has cloned the repository check a release
# without asking GitHub to vouch for it — which is the point, in a project whose
# claim is that you can read what you are running.
#
# `namespaces="git"` restricts the key to git signatures, so it cannot be used
# to verify a signature made for some other purpose with the same key.
#
# Rotating a key means ADDING a line, never replacing one. Tags already signed
# with an old key must keep verifying, and the tag ruleset blocks deletion and
# non-fast-forward, so those signatures can never be redone. tools/check_signers.py
# fails if any existing tag stops verifying, which is what makes that concrete.
#
# pii-scan: allow the maintainer's own address, which authors every commit here already and is the identity a signature is checked against
